8. The right to welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) letter g, includes:a. the right to have welfare, either alone or together; andb. the right to perform work and receive fair wages in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
9. The right to participate in government as referred to in paragraph (1) letter h, includes:a. the right to be elected and to vote in general elections;
b. the right to pursue a career in government; andc. the right to convey aspirations directly or through intermediaries in government.

Article 28

1. Every woman in the Regency Area as a witness and victim as referred to in Article 27 letter a, has the right to:a. obtain protection for the security of herself, Family, and property, and be free from threats related to testimony that will be, is being, or has been given;
b. participate in the process of selecting and determining the form of protection and security support;
c. provide testimony without pressure;
d. obtain an interpreter;
e. be free from ensnaring questions;
f. obtain information regarding case developments;
g. obtain information regarding court decisions; h. obtain information in the event the convict is released;
i. have identity kept confidential;
j. obtain a new identity;
k. obtain temporary residence;
l. obtain a new residence;
m. obtain reimbursement of transportation costs according to needs;
n. obtain legal advice;
o. obtain temporary living cost assistance until the protection period ends; and/orp. obtain assistance.
2. Women in the Regency Area as victims of gross human rights violations, victims of terrorism crimes, victims of human trafficking crimes, victims of torture crimes, victims of sexual violence crimes, and victims of severe persecution, in addition to being entitled to the rights as referred to in paragraph (1), are also entitled to receive:a. medical assistance:b. psychosocial and psychological rehabilitation assistance;
c. compensation; andd. restitution.
3. Restitution as referred to in paragraph (2) letter d, is in the form of:a. compensation for loss of wealth or income;
b. compensation arising from suffering directly related as a result of the crime; and/orc. reimbursement of medical and/or psychological treatment costs.
4. The granting of rights to women as witnesses and victims as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations governing the protection of witnesses and victims.

Article 62

1. Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 61, is provided to:a. Children in emergency situations;
b. Children in conflict with the law;
c. Children from minority and isolated groups;
d. Children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
e. Children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. Children who are victims of pornography;
g. Children with HIV and AIDS;
h. Children victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking;
i. Children victims of bullying;
j. Children victims of sexual crimes;
k. Children victims of terrorism networks;
l. Children with disabilities;
m. Children victims of abuse and neglect;
n. Children with deviant social behavior; ando. Children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
2. Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out through efforts:a. rapid handling, including treatment and/or physical, psychological, and social rehabilitation, as well as prevention of diseases and other health disorders;
b. psychosocial assistance during treatment until recovery;
c. provision of social assistance for Children from underprivileged Families; andd. provision of protection and assistance in every judicial process.
3. Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1) is provided at UPTD/UPTD PPA and Puspaga.
4. Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented quickly, comprehensively, and in an integrated manner.

Article 31

1. The Regent coordinates the implementation of Protection of Women from GBV in disasters.
2. Coordination of the implementation of Protection of Women from GBV in disasters as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out by:a. ensuring the existence of programs and activities carried out by Regency Regional Apparatus regarding the protection of women's rights from GBV in disasters;
b. ensuring the fulfillment of specific needs of women including persons with disabilities and the elderly;
c. monitoring the development of services related to victim handling;
d. building a mechanism for implementing monitoring, evaluation, and reporting of Protection of Women from GBV services in disasters periodically and in stages; ande. preparing an action plan for Protection of Women from GBV in disasters.

Article 40

1. Achievement of KTP prevention involving parties in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 37 letter a is measured by criteria.
2. Criteria for achievement of KTP prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:a. compliance with implementation of policies regarding KTP prevention;
b. impact of implementation of programs, activities, and budget regarding KTP prevention;
c. impact of communication, information, and education activities regarding KTP prevention;
d. synergy of programs related to KTP prevention;
e. integration of KTP prevention into Regency regional development planning; and f. Community Participation and cooperation with the Business World and mass media in prevention

Part Six Protection of Women with Disabilities

Article 34

1. Protection of women with disabilities is carried out through the fulfillment of women's rights as persons with disabilities.
2. The rights of women as persons with disabilities as referred to in paragraph (1), include:a. life;
b. freedom from stigma;
c. privacy;
d. justice and legal protection;
e. education;
f. employment, entrepreneurship, and cooperatives;
g. health;
h. politics;
i. religion;
j. sports;
k. culture and tourism;
l. social welfare;
m. accessibility;
n. public services;
o. protection from disasters;
p. habilitation and rehabilitation;
q. Concessions;
r. data collection;
s. living independently and being involved in society;
t. expressing, communicating, and obtaining information;
u. moving and citizenship; andv. freedom from acts of Discrimination, neglect, torture, and Exploitation.
3. In addition to having the rights of Women as persons with disabilities as referred to in paragraph (2), women with disabilities have the right:a. to reproductive health;
b. to accept or reject the use of contraceptives;
c. to obtain greater protection from multiple Discrimination; andd. to obtain greater protection from acts of Violence, including sexual Violence and Exploitation.

Part Three Fulfillment of Children's Rights

Paragraph 1 General

Article 52

1. The Regency Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility in the implementation of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights.
2. The Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:a. the right to live, grow, develop, and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity, and to receive protection from violence and Discrimination;
b. the right to a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
c. the right to worship according to their religion, think, and express themselves in accordance with their level of intelligence and age under the guidance of Parents or Guardians;
d. the right to know their Parents, be raised, and be cared for by their own Parents;
e. the right to be cared for or adopted as a foster Child or adopted Child by others in the event that for any reason the Parents cannot guarantee the growth and development of the Child, or the Child is in a state of neglect;
f. the right to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs;
g. the right to obtain education and teaching in the context of developing their personality and level of intelligence in accordance with their interests and talents;h. the right to receive protection in educational units from sexual crimes and Violence committed by educators, education personnel, fellow students, and/or other parties;
i. the right to express and have their opinion heard, receive, seek, and provide information in accordance with their level of intelligence and age for their self-development in accordance with the values of decency and propriety;
j. the right to rest and utilize leisure time, socialize with children of the same age, play, recreate, and create in accordance with their interests, talents, and level of intelligence for self-development;
k. the right to receive protection from Discrimination, Exploitation, both economic and sexual, neglect, cruelty, violence, and abuse, injustice, and other mistreatment while in the care of Parents, Guardians, or any other party responsible for care;
1. the right to be cared for by their own Parents, unless there are valid reasons and/or legal rules showing that the separation and/or divorce is in the best interests of the Child and is a last consideration;
m. the right to meet directly and maintain regular personal contact with both Parents, receive care, maintenance, education and protection for the growth and development process from both Parents in accordance with their abilities, talents, and interests, obtain living expenses from both Parents, and obtain other Children's Rights, in the event of separation and/or divorce as referred to in letter 1;
n. the right to obtain protection from abuse in political activities, involvement in armed conflicts, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of Violence, involvement in warfare, and sexual crimes;
o. the right to obtain protection from being targets of persecution, torture, or imposition of inhuman punishment, and to obtain freedom in accordance with the law;
p. the right to receive humane treatment and placement separated from adults, obtain legal assistance or other assistance effectively at every stage of applicable legal efforts, and defend themselves and obtain justice before an objective and impartial juvenile court in a closed session to the public, in the case of a Child deprived of liberty;
q. the right to confidentiality in the case of a Child who becomes a victim or perpetrator of sexual Violence or who is in conflict with the law;
r. the right to receive legal assistance and other assistance in the event that a child becomes a victim or perpetrator of a criminal act; ands. the right to receive protection and maintenance after divorce.
3. The implementation of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), is carried out in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations governing the protection of Children.

Article 71

Prevention of VAC involving parties in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 70 letter a shall be implemented through:
a. implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the prevention of VAC involving parties in the Regency Area;
b. provision of education on VAC prevention activities involving parties in the Regency Area;
c. enhancement of the participation of the Community, Business World, media, and Children in the prevention of VAC in the Regency Area; andd. strengthening cooperation to realize CFC, child-friendly sub-districts, and child-friendly Villages/sub-districts.

Article 32

The Regent coordinates the Protection of Women from GBV in disasters in the Regency Area, as referred to in Article 31 paragraph (1), carried out by:
a. providing information and Disaggregated Data based on gender, age, and type of GBV vulnerability;
b. building stakeholder commitment to prevention, handling, and Protection of Women from GBV in disasters;
c. providing women-friendly space facilities, as well as Gender Responsive facilities and infrastructure in emergency response and rehabilitation conditions that accommodate local resources;
d. providing complaint, handling, and referral services needed by women victims of Violence that accommodate local wisdom; ande. ensuring victims or survivors receive empowerment services according to their potential, abilities, and needs so they can recover quickly, become independent, and productive.

Article 47

1. Strengthening and development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 37 letter c shall be implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
2. The standards for strengthening and development as referred to in paragraph (1) include:a. measurable and certified human resource capacity of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions for victims of Violence in the Regency Area;
b. specific needs for women in emergency situations and special conditions in the Regency Area that are on target and appropriate;
c. effective and efficient communication, information, and education strategies regarding the protection of women's rights;
d. networks among Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area;
e. Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area have agreed service standards and operational system procedures; andf. Measurable development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area.
3. The procedures for strengthening and development as referred to in paragraph (1) include:a. policy advocacy and assistance in providing service facilities and infrastructure for women victims of Violence in the Regency Area;
b. capacity building of human resources of service provider institutions for handling women victims of Violence in the Regency Area;
c. provision of specific needs for women in emergency situations and special conditions in the Regency Area; andd. strengthening networks among Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area.

Article 43

1. Provision of services for women victims of Violence requiring coordination in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 37 letter b is implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
2. Standards for provision of services for women victims of Violence requiring coordination in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1) include:a. receiving reports and/or outreach to victims of Violence;
b. providing information about the rights of victims of Violence;
c. facilitating provision of health services;
d. facilitating provision of psychological strengthening services;
e. facilitating provision of psychosocial services, Social Rehabilitation, social empowerment, Repatriation, and Social Reintegration;
f. providing legal services;
g. identifying economic empowerment needs;
h. identifying temporary shelter needs for victims of Violence and Families of victims of Violence that need to be fulfilled immediately;
i. facilitating needs of Victims of Violence with disabilities;
j. coordination and cooperation on fulfillment of rights of victims of Violence with other institutions; andk. monitoring fulfillment of rights of victims of Violence by law enforcement apparatus during judicial proceedings.
3. Procedures for provision of services as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:a. provision of community complaint services for women victims of Violence in the Regency Area, covering:
1. establishment of UPTD PPA in the Regency Area;
2. implementation of UPTD PPA services; and
3. provision and strengthening of human resources for service providers through training and certification of Women's rights protection services.  b. coordination and synchronization of implementation of provision of follow-up referral services for women victims of violence in the Regency Area, covering:
1. cross-coordination of Regency Regional Apparatus for implementation of provision of KTP case services;
2. policy development and implementation of integrated KTP case handling system across related Regency Regional Apparatus; and
3. data collection, monitoring, and evaluation of KTP case handling.

