Part Two Fulfillment of Women's Rights

Article 22

1. Fulfillment of the rights of every Woman in the Regency Area, includes:a. the right to life;
b. the right to form a family and continue offspring;
c. the right to self-development;
d. the right to obtain justice;
e. the right to personal freedom;
f. the right to security;
g. the right to welfare; andh. the right to participate in government.
2. The right to life as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a, includes:a. the right to maintain life and improve one's standard of living;
b. the right to obtain peace of mind, security, peace, happiness, physical and spiritual welfare; andc. the right to a good and healthy environment.
3. The right to form a family and continue offspring as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, namely the right to form a Family and continue offspring through lawful marriage in accordance with religion and the provisions of laws and regulations governing marriage.
4. The right to self-development as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, includes:a. the right to fulfillment of basic needs to grow and develop properly;
b. the right to protection for personal development, to obtain education, educate oneself and improve quality of life in order to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
c. the right to develop and obtain Benefits from science and technology, arts and culture in accordance with human dignity for the welfare of oneself, the nation, and humanity;
d. the right to communicate and obtain information necessary to develop oneself and one's social environment in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
e. the right to seek, obtain, own, store, process, and convey information using all types of available means in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
f. the right to fight for the right to self-development, both individually and collectively, to build society, nation, and state in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
g. the right to perform social work in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
h. the right to participate in the planning and implementation of government policies in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
i. the right to organize and form organizations in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations; andj. the right to organize education and teaching in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms.
5. The right to obtain justice as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d, includes:a. the right to receive equal treatment without Discrimination;
b. the right to obtain justice;
c. the right to submit applications, complaints, and lawsuits, whether in criminal, civil, or administrative cases; andd. the right to be tried through a free and impartial judicial process, in accordance with procedural law that guarantees objective examination by honest and fair judges to obtain a just and correct decision.
6. The right to personal freedom as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e, includes:a. the right not to be enslaved or subjugated, trafficked, and all acts with similar purposes;
b. the right to personal integrity, both spiritual and physical;
c. the right not to be the object of research without one's consent; andd. the right to freely express and convey opinions with due regard to religious values, morality, order, public interest, and national integrity.
7. The right to security as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f, includes:a. the right to protection of oneself, Family, honor, dignity, and property rights;
b. the right to recognition before the law as a human person; andc. the right to be free from threats to do or not do something that violates religious, social, and legal norms.
8. The right to welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) letter g, includes:a. the right to have welfare, either alone or together; andb. the right to perform work and receive fair wages in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
9. The right to participate in government as referred to in paragraph (1) letter h, includes:a. the right to be elected and to vote in general elections;
b. the right to pursue a career in government; andc. the right to convey aspirations directly or through intermediaries in government.

Article 4

This Regional Regulation aims to:
a. realize Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in the Regency Area that is effective, efficient, optimal, transparent, and accountable, as well as administratively orderly;
b. realize Gender Responsive development by integrating Gender Perspective into planning, budgeting, implementation, monitoring, evaluation, supervision, and reporting on policies, programs, activities and sub-activities of development in the Regency Area;
c. realize Gender Equality in family, community, national and state life;
d. realize Gender Responsive Regional budget management;
e. support efforts to increase the capacity of forums, institutions and/or organizations for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;
f. increase the synergy of Regency Regional Government and the community in the development of Gender Responsive Women's Empowerment and Child Protection; andg. increase Community Participation in the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in the Regency Area.

Article 32

The Regent coordinates the Protection of Women from GBV in disasters in the Regency Area, as referred to in Article 31 paragraph (1), carried out by:
a. providing information and Disaggregated Data based on gender, age, and type of GBV vulnerability;
