Part Four Scope

Article 5

The scope of this Regional Regulation regulates:
a. Quality of Life of Women;
b. Protection of Women;
c. Protection of Children;
d. Improvement of Family Quality;
e. Gender and Child Data System;
f. cooperation;
g. Community Participation;
h. coordination;
i. monitoring;
j. reporting;
k. awards;
l. funding; andm. sanctions.

Article 32

The Regent coordinates the Protection of Women from GBV in disasters in the Regency Area, as referred to in Article 31 paragraph (1), carried out by:
a. providing information and Disaggregated Data based on gender, age, and type of GBV vulnerability;
b. building stakeholder commitment to prevention, handling, and Protection of Women from GBV in disasters;
c. providing women-friendly space facilities, as well as Gender Responsive facilities and infrastructure in emergency response and rehabilitation conditions that accommodate local resources;
d. providing complaint, handling, and referral services needed by women victims of Violence that accommodate local wisdom; ande. ensuring victims or survivors receive empowerment services according to their potential, abilities, and needs so they can recover quickly, become independent, and productive.

Article 28

1. Every woman in the Regency Area as a witness and victim as referred to in Article 27 letter a, has the right to:a. obtain protection for the security of herself, Family, and property, and be free from threats related to testimony that will be, is being, or has been given;
b. participate in the process of selecting and determining the form of protection and security support;
c. provide testimony without pressure;
d. obtain an interpreter;
e. be free from ensnaring questions;
f. obtain information regarding case developments;
g. obtain information regarding court decisions; h. obtain information in the event the convict is released;
i. have identity kept confidential;
j. obtain a new identity;
k. obtain temporary residence;
l. obtain a new residence;
m. obtain reimbursement of transportation costs according to needs;
n. obtain legal advice;
o. obtain temporary living cost assistance until the protection period ends; and/orp. obtain assistance.
2. Women in the Regency Area as victims of gross human rights violations, victims of terrorism crimes, victims of human trafficking crimes, victims of torture crimes, victims of sexual violence crimes, and victims of severe persecution, in addition to being entitled to the rights as referred to in paragraph (1), are also entitled to receive:a. medical assistance:b. psychosocial and psychological rehabilitation assistance;
c. compensation; andd. restitution.
3. Restitution as referred to in paragraph (2) letter d, is in the form of:a. compensation for loss of wealth or income;
b. compensation arising from suffering directly related as a result of the crime; and/orc. reimbursement of medical and/or psychological treatment costs.
4. The granting of rights to women as witnesses and victims as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations governing the protection of witnesses and victims.

Article 24

1. The Regency Regional Government undertakes prevention efforts as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (3) letter a, to avoid Women from the impacts of Conflict situations and events.
